WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:04.003
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Oscar Isaac.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:07.240
Like a lot of
Juilliard graduates,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.442
you've already managed to
mention that you went there.

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:10.877
-Yup. [ Laughter ]

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:13.980
Number one rule of Juilliard,
always talk about Juilliard.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.749
-But you were
writing and performing in

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.017
your own plays as a kid.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.585
This made sense.

00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:20.719
-I did. I did.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:23.689
Yeah, I wrote play,
a musical in fifth grade

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:25.257
where I played a platypus.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:26.726
I went to a Christian school.
-Uh-huh.

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:29.062
-And so it was about Noah's ark.
-Oh, wow.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.263
So you played a --

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:32.599
It was Noah's ark from
a platypus's perspective?

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.967
-Yeah, from a doubtful platypus,
because he just didn't buy it.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:36.703
-Oh, that's a very good take.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.004
-Yeah, I had to
have some conflict.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.372
-For a fifth grader, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.474
-So all the other animals
were on board but the platypus?

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:44.210
-The platypus was like, "Uh-uh,
I don't believe this crap."

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.644
And then he drowns.

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:47.179
[ Laughter ]
-Does he really?

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:48.481
-That's what God does.

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.117
-Oh, wow.
-No, no, he doesn't drown.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.953
He, luckily, you know, made a --

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.789
-Yeah, I bet at the last
minute, he was like, "Oh, yeah."

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:58.491
Because I will say, that's
a risky animal to say,

00:00:58.491 --> 00:00:59.491
"I'm not coming."

00:00:59.491 --> 00:01:00.526
Because the way
a platypus looks,

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:02.595
I think they're like,
"We're fine.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.031
I think we can be fine
without a platypus."

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:06.933
-Right, right. Yeah.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.736
-And you made
your own films as well?

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:10.970
-Yeah.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.739
Yeah, yeah, yeah,
as I showed at the "SNL" thing.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:13.973
-Yeah.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.008
-I was really into
karate movies, so I would

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:18.077
do a lot of videos of me
just like, kicking my friends.

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:19.379
-That's great.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.748
-You know, I was very lucky.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:23.850
I feel like my parents
loved movies.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:24.884
They loved comedy.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.019
They liked sharing it
with their kids.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.020
-Yeah.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.121
-Your dad is the same way,
right?

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:29.522
-Big time, yeah.
He would always, you know,

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.391
this was when
we had a Betamax.

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:33.293
He'd come home every Friday
night with, like,

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:35.761
a brown case
with a new movie in it.

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:40.666
So the video store was always
this, like, amazing Mecca.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:43.669
And we had one down the road
called "Hooked on Videos,"

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:44.970
and I'd go there all the time.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.741
And they had a "Street Fighter
II" arcade game too,

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.677
where I would always
play there with my friend.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:55.548
And I got to know the guy, so
he would let me get videos

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.751
that I definitely shouldn't have
been getting at 11 years old.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.354
-What's an
age-inappropriate movie

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:02.422
you watched as an 11-year-old?

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:03.856
-"Deer Hunter."
-Yeah, that's not --

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:05.024
-Probably not.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.792
-Almost no one is ever
like, "Kids section?

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:08.494
Where do we put it?"

00:02:08.494 --> 00:02:11.197
-Yeah. It was right
next to "Garfield".

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.132
Yeah, that really
made an impression,

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:15.101
that whole Chris Walken
Russian roulette scene.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:16.502
-Did you find --

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:18.271
because I feel like because
my parents introduced me to,

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:21.273
especially on the comedy side,
more adult comedy,

00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:23.743
it was very hard for me
to watch comedy for kids.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:26.312
I feel like I outgrew that
faster than my peers.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.081
-Yeah, I think after
"Deer Hunter,"

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.783
"Transformers" was a little
difficult to go back to.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:31.917
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:33.252
You're like, "They're not
risking anything."

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:34.820
-Where's
the Russian roulette scene

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.088
with Optimus Prime?

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:40.326
-Right.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:41.494
We've talked,
again, about singing.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.528
You were in a band.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.364
-I was.
-Can you verify --

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:48.334
Your band did pretty well.
Opened for Green Day.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.969
-Technically.
-Okay.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.237
[ Applause ]

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.471
-We played --

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:53.740
-They're clapping too
much for "technically."

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.374
-We played --
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:57.210
Hey, it's more than
a lot of people did.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.411
-It's more than a lot of
people, that's right.

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.013
-We played at the same
festival on the same day.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:02.748
-Okay.
-A couple hours apart.

00:03:02.748 --> 00:03:04.950
A few hours apart.
-Same stage?

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.152
-Maybe not the same stage.

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.553
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:09.088
I don't remember exactly.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.223
-I feel like you'd remember
if it was the same stage.

00:03:11.223 --> 00:03:12.458
-Maybe not the same stage.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:14.527
It may have been
a trailer with like,

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:16.028
a little, tiny,
like, scaffolding on it.

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:17.429
-Gotcha.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:19.231
So they were on a stage, and
you were on a moving trailer

00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:20.466
that pulled in the background.

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.468
-At the same time.

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:25.471
-And somebody yelled,
"Keep it down!"

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:28.508
So you mentioned you
went to a Christian school.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.643
Your family, you had
a very religious upbringing.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.079
-I did. How about you?
-No, not at all.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:34.113
-No?
-Yeah.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.381
-Just non-believing?

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.216
-Yeah, we were just
fully non-believing.

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:40.319
The rest of the neighborhood
called us the platypuses.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.021
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.489
-Uh-huh. But now!

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.858
-But now, we're like,
"Oh, no, no, no."

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.193
-Yeah, yeah.
-How did that manifest?

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:50.296
Were there things that
you were taught as a kid

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:52.999
that seem weird now,
that you had to outgrow?

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:54.567
-[ Laughing ] Yeah, all of it.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.235
All of it was weird.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:59.004
-And a form of spiritual abuse,
I would say.

00:03:59.004 --> 00:04:01.807
No, look, there were some
really beautiful things as well.

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:02.942
But, yeah, you know.

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:05.478
I think there's
a lot of commonality

00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:07.713
with a lot of immigrant families
that, you know,

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:10.182
come to this country,
and they find their community

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:13.853
with these kind of
very religious groups.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.254
But it was weird.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:18.858
It was weird to grow up with
exorcisms at home sometimes.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:21.927
-Like, people would come over
them for them?

00:04:21.927 --> 00:04:25.031
-Yeah, like, I remember them
exorcising my grandma.

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:26.098
-Oh, wow!

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:27.033
-Not like this kind of
exercising.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.234
It's like, yeah.

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:30.569
-And did you at the time,
as a kid think, like,

00:04:30.569 --> 00:04:31.637
"Yeah, they should.

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:35.408
Grandma could use it."

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:37.110
-"Grandma needs an exorcism.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.345
I feel like she's been
really annoying lately."

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.113
-Yeah, I mean, like,
let's at least give it a try.

00:04:41.113 --> 00:04:42.849
It can't get worse.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:44.083
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.719
-Yeah, pretty much.
Pretty much.

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:51.490
It was a lot of weird stuff.
-You grew up in Florida.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.893
Weird jobs before you found
your path to being an actor?

00:04:54.893 --> 00:04:55.928
-Not many.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.730
Never really been able
to do much else.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.531
I worked at a golf club
for a couple of months.

00:04:59.531 --> 00:05:00.766
-Uh-huh.

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:02.235
-I worked in a movie theater
for a couple days.

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:03.670
-Okay.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.705
-The longest job I had was as
a transporter at a hospital.

00:05:06.705 --> 00:05:08.674
-Okay.
How did you get a job like that?

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:10.777
-Because my dad worked
at the hospital as a doctor.

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:12.878
-Okay. So he got you a job?
-He got me a job.

00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:14.012
Right after high school,

00:05:14.012 --> 00:05:15.414
I was going to
community college in Miami

00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:17.249
and working as a transporter.

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:18.984
So I would take, like,
bodies to the morgue.

00:05:18.984 --> 00:05:20.586
-Wow!
-Yeah.

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:21.954
-That seems like --

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.890
I guess I don't know why I'm
surprised that would be

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.893
something that somebody
who had just got hired did.

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:28.360
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.029
But then, I guess
on the other hand...

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:31.697
-You're not going to hurt them.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:32.765
[ Laughter ]
-You know?

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:34.533
-That's a good point.
-Yeah, right?

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:37.537
You know what was interesting,
is that they would put them --

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:39.071
they had
a very special stretcher.

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:40.806
It looks like
a regular stretcher,

00:05:40.806 --> 00:05:42.108
but you'd put
the body underneath

00:05:42.108 --> 00:05:43.575
so nobody would know.
-Oh.

00:05:43.575 --> 00:05:44.777
-When you're, like,
wheeling it around.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.846
-So like room service food.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.681
It was like, to keep it warm.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:49.982
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.750
-That was my first job,
actually.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.285
Like, when I arrived
my first day.

00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:54.654
-Uh-huh.

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:56.555
-They were like, "Alright,
you're going to go with Sam

00:05:56.555 --> 00:05:58.590
to bring the body
down to the morgue."

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.392
-How many would you --

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.261
Now this is a weird question
that sounds like

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:03.495
this is what I've wanted
to ask the whole time

00:06:03.495 --> 00:06:04.597
you've been out here.

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:05.865
-How many bodies?

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:06.999
-How many would you say
you brought down there?

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:08.901
-"How many we talking about?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:11.170
Like, more than 50?"
-Would you say more than 50?

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.905
-What did they smell like?

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:15.474
-Would you like to hang out with
some like-minded people?

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:19.111
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.980
If I had a Facebook group,
would you join?

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.648
Anyway, um --

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:23.916
I think the rest of
this conversation,

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.351
we should have off camera.

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.786
I want to say how lovely it is
to have you here, as always.

00:06:27.786 --> 00:06:29.789
Thank you so much, you guys.
-Thank you.

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.725
-Oscar Isaac, "Moon Knight,"
streaming now on Disney Plus.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:33.726
We'll be right back.

